WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.253 --> 00:00:01.993
So I'm excited to introduce our guest,

00:00:01.993 --> 00:00:04.380
Dr. Jharett Bryantt,
Jharett, are you there?

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.243
I'm sorry for the technical difficulties.

00:00:06.243 --> 00:00:09.013
My internet connection has
been spotty, (chuckles)

00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:11.196
Let's just call it that, Jharett-

00:00:12.543 --> 00:00:14.676
- Good to see you too, Sal, how are you?

00:00:15.553 --> 00:00:16.386
- I'm doing great.

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:18.833
It's been a little while since
we saw each other in Houston.

00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:21.313
Oh man. Might've been a year
and a half, two years ago,

00:00:21.313 --> 00:00:23.043
but good to see you.

00:00:23.043 --> 00:00:24.833
- Yeah, no, it's a pleasure to be here.

00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:26.693
Like I said, I want to shout you out.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.693
Like I'm one of your biggest fans.

00:00:28.693 --> 00:00:32.393
So I've been using Khan
Academy since forever.

00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:34.413
I think you literally taught me calculus

00:00:34.413 --> 00:00:37.093
when I had to take my
teacher certification exam

00:00:37.093 --> 00:00:37.926
way back when

00:00:37.926 --> 00:00:40.833
I had to refresh on some
of these difficult topics

00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:43.393
and I always had Khan
Academy there to help me out.

00:00:43.393 --> 00:00:44.483
So it's been huge,

00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:47.426
and I kind of took that
with me to my work in HISV.

00:00:48.593 --> 00:00:50.993
- Well, it's exciting you'd mentioned that

00:00:50.993 --> 00:00:51.863
the first time we met

00:00:51.863 --> 00:00:53.903
and I was kind of blown away that I guess

00:00:53.903 --> 00:00:57.663
I am now so old that people who
have been using Khan Academy

00:00:57.663 --> 00:01:01.003
are now Assistant Superintendents
of major school districts.

00:01:01.003 --> 00:01:04.473
But I think you're also
unusually precocious

00:01:04.473 --> 00:01:07.003
in your career, and you're-

00:01:07.003 --> 00:01:07.853
but that's flattering.

00:01:07.853 --> 00:01:10.173
And you know, that's the exact hope of why

00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:11.543
I've always wanted to do Khan Academy,

00:01:11.543 --> 00:01:13.143
and why I wanted to focus on education

00:01:13.143 --> 00:01:15.683
because if you can empower other folks,

00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:16.803
they then can they go on.

00:01:16.803 --> 00:01:18.450
and empower others to
do incredible things,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.523
and I think you're an
incredible example of that.

00:01:21.523 --> 00:01:23.733
Houston ISD for those who don't know,

00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:25.183
I believe it's the fourth largest

00:01:25.183 --> 00:01:26.993
or third largest school
district in America.

00:01:26.993 --> 00:01:28.703
It's one of the top five, I think

00:01:29.613 --> 00:01:31.633
- Seventh largest in the country.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.793
- Okay, I was close, seventh
largest in the country.

00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:37.683
I'm curious, you know, and
there's a lot to talk about.

00:01:37.683 --> 00:01:39.453
I want to talk about
post-secondary success

00:01:39.453 --> 00:01:41.183
and some of the things
y'all been seeing there,

00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:43.973
but given the fact that
we're continuing through

00:01:43.973 --> 00:01:46.003
the COVID period,

00:01:46.003 --> 00:01:47.983
and this has been incredibly hard on,

00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:50.683
I could imagine you and your colleagues.

00:01:50.683 --> 00:01:52.503
How have you all been dealing with it?

00:01:52.503 --> 00:01:53.336
Take us through,

00:01:53.336 --> 00:01:55.613
what's been going on for all
of y'all and what's been going

00:01:55.613 --> 00:01:59.253
on through for the
district since last spring.

00:01:59.253 --> 00:02:01.353
And how have you all navigated the crisis.

00:02:02.443 --> 00:02:04.413
- Yeah, well, fortunately,

00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:07.443
I work alongside thousands
of incredible colleagues,

00:02:07.443 --> 00:02:10.763
tens of thousands of amazing
teachers our senior leadership

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.963
our superintendent our trustees.

00:02:12.963 --> 00:02:14.903
And in the middle of a crisis,

00:02:14.903 --> 00:02:17.003
we all rolled up our sleeves
and did whatever we could

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:17.836
for students.

00:02:17.836 --> 00:02:20.293
So, and obviously we shut down the schools

00:02:20.293 --> 00:02:22.933
in March and we were virtual
for quite a long time.

00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:26.603
We transitioned back to
in-person learning, but you know,

00:02:26.603 --> 00:02:30.246
going virtual in the middle
of the spring semester,

00:02:31.103 --> 00:02:33.873
was really eye-opening
for me because you realize

00:02:33.873 --> 00:02:37.263
how fragile some of the processes
are that exist out there

00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:39.033
to get our students into higher education.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:42.203
And that happens to be
my work in Houston ISD.

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:45.213
Is focusing on what can we
do to get students college

00:02:45.213 --> 00:02:46.743
credit while in high school?

00:02:46.743 --> 00:02:47.803
What can we do to ensure

00:02:47.803 --> 00:02:50.293
that students received college counseling?

00:02:50.293 --> 00:02:52.083
That's amazing, that's effective.

00:02:52.083 --> 00:02:54.823
That'll take them to where they
want to be wherever that is.

00:02:54.823 --> 00:02:56.133
And of course, when you go virtual,

00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:57.533
that's all in jeopardy.

00:02:57.533 --> 00:02:59.643
But I will say that the great thing

00:02:59.643 --> 00:03:01.353
about working in Houston ISD

00:03:01.353 --> 00:03:04.173
is that we're very forward-thinking.

00:03:04.173 --> 00:03:05.703
We've been thinking about online learning

00:03:05.703 --> 00:03:07.343
for a very long time,

00:03:07.343 --> 00:03:08.983
even before my time in HRSD,

00:03:08.983 --> 00:03:11.213
which has been about eight years.

00:03:11.213 --> 00:03:14.063
All of our high school
students had laptops.

00:03:14.063 --> 00:03:15.933
We had been talking about what they call

00:03:15.933 --> 00:03:18.073
the flipped classroom or blended learning,

00:03:18.073 --> 00:03:19.813
which is a fancy way to say,

00:03:19.813 --> 00:03:21.683
you can use your laptop to study

00:03:21.683 --> 00:03:23.693
for class before you even go to school.

00:03:23.693 --> 00:03:26.303
We'd be talking about these
things for a very long time,

00:03:26.303 --> 00:03:30.473
as you know, so we're very
huge fans of Khan Academy.

00:03:30.473 --> 00:03:31.306
In fact,

00:03:31.306 --> 00:03:34.463
we had a series of campaigns
over the last few years called

00:03:34.463 --> 00:03:38.454
the You Khan Do It campaign
or your Khan Athlons,

00:03:38.454 --> 00:03:41.453
because we really believe in
virtual learning and everything

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:43.283
that comes with it to
supplement what's going on

00:03:43.283 --> 00:03:44.233
in the classroom.

00:03:44.233 --> 00:03:46.053
So even though it was
challenging for everybody

00:03:46.053 --> 00:03:48.503
in the country, I would
say that it was really

00:03:50.053 --> 00:03:53.403
quite the experiment to kind
of use all these tools we've

00:03:53.403 --> 00:03:54.803
been building for years and putting them

00:03:54.803 --> 00:03:55.993
to the the real task,

00:03:55.993 --> 00:03:57.783
which is making sure that
our students are ready

00:03:57.783 --> 00:03:58.973
to move forward,

00:03:58.973 --> 00:04:01.263
to go beyond and achieve
their dreams regardless

00:04:01.263 --> 00:04:02.166
of any crisis.

00:04:03.617 --> 00:04:05.223
- And, you mentioned you're kind of,

00:04:05.223 --> 00:04:09.183
one of your focal areas is
preparedness for college.

00:04:09.183 --> 00:04:10.533
What have you seen you?

00:04:10.533 --> 00:04:12.363
I was listening to the radio this morning

00:04:12.363 --> 00:04:14.293
and they're talking about studies around,

00:04:14.293 --> 00:04:16.473
the number of kids who were
enrolling in college this year

00:04:16.473 --> 00:04:18.623
is 20% lower than what we see

00:04:18.623 --> 00:04:20.903
in normal years, and
it's disproportionately,

00:04:20.903 --> 00:04:22.983
I believe the number for low income kids

00:04:22.983 --> 00:04:25.533
or kids from historically
under-resourced groups

00:04:25.533 --> 00:04:29.343
is actually at 30% lower
rates of college enrollment.

00:04:29.343 --> 00:04:30.603
Are y'all seeing something similar

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.956
from the graduates of of Houston ISD?

00:04:34.023 --> 00:04:35.603
- So we're looking at that data too,

00:04:35.603 --> 00:04:37.833
those are nationwide trends
and there are a lot of reasons

00:04:37.833 --> 00:04:40.063
for that more so than ever before.

00:04:40.063 --> 00:04:41.273
We're hearing on the ground

00:04:41.273 --> 00:04:44.280
that students are having
real thoughts about,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.703
"Do I need to earn money
to support my family,

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:48.263
or do I go to school?

00:04:48.263 --> 00:04:50.093
Do I go to a four year that's outside

00:04:50.093 --> 00:04:51.923
of the very large state of Texas?

00:04:51.923 --> 00:04:53.573
Or do I stay somewhere more local?"

00:04:53.573 --> 00:04:55.483
Maybe go to an open enrollment college

00:04:55.483 --> 00:05:00.223
that, ultimately will change
their entire life trajectory.

00:05:00.223 --> 00:05:03.503
But we had a lot of bright
spots, even in the myths,

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:06.096
the COVID pandemic in March.

00:05:06.953 --> 00:05:09.373
I support a very special
program called Emerge,

00:05:09.373 --> 00:05:12.833
which focuses on our high
achieving low income students.

00:05:12.833 --> 00:05:16.053
And we work with them to get
into highly selective schools.

00:05:16.053 --> 00:05:18.363
And one of the things that
makes that program great

00:05:18.363 --> 00:05:20.323
is that we invest in these students

00:05:20.323 --> 00:05:23.133
and we send them all on an
out-of-state college tour.

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:25.843
And we had a cohort of
students that weren't able

00:05:25.843 --> 00:05:28.003
to attend an out of state college tour.

00:05:28.003 --> 00:05:30.863
We had a lot of students that
have a lot of anxiety around,

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:33.373
how are they going to
make it to the finish line

00:05:33.373 --> 00:05:36.453
and get to these highly selective
schools that are far away.

00:05:36.453 --> 00:05:39.643
And for that program, our
numbers were unchanged.

00:05:39.643 --> 00:05:42.693
We had actually a higher
percentage of students enrolled at

00:05:42.693 --> 00:05:45.883
highly selective universities
all over the country,

00:05:45.883 --> 00:05:48.943
but that's because we
made strategic investments

00:05:48.943 --> 00:05:52.403
into students that really needed
an extra layer of support,

00:05:52.403 --> 00:05:55.253
but it doesn't always look
like that for other districts

00:05:55.253 --> 00:05:56.553
and schools across the nation.

00:05:56.553 --> 00:05:58.583
So we're feeling the pinch too,

00:05:58.583 --> 00:06:00.923
but we're really trying
to roll up our sleeves

00:06:00.923 --> 00:06:03.753
and figure out, well, what
can we do to change that?

00:06:03.753 --> 00:06:06.043
Because we want students
to have all the options

00:06:06.043 --> 00:06:07.693
that will help them achieve whatever

00:06:07.693 --> 00:06:08.923
their life condition is,

00:06:08.923 --> 00:06:11.013
whether it's two or four
year college or whatever

00:06:11.013 --> 00:06:11.846
that may be.

00:06:12.823 --> 00:06:13.733
- Yeah. And on both fronts,

00:06:13.733 --> 00:06:16.033
kind of the COVID and the
getting ready for college front

00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:18.053
post-secondary success side,

00:06:18.053 --> 00:06:20.373
it looks like we have another six months

00:06:20.373 --> 00:06:22.018
of weirdness. I think,

00:06:22.018 --> 00:06:23.863
we're finally seeing the
light at the end of the tunnel

00:06:23.863 --> 00:06:25.873
with hope around a vaccine.

00:06:25.873 --> 00:06:28.183
How are you at the district
thinking about that?

00:06:28.183 --> 00:06:29.923
What are the conversations
you're having both in terms of,

00:06:29.923 --> 00:06:31.563
how do you keep kids learning?

00:06:31.563 --> 00:06:33.783
How do you help fill in any gaps for kids

00:06:33.783 --> 00:06:36.903
that might've been disengaged
during the pandemic?

00:06:36.903 --> 00:06:38.083
And then how do you ensure

00:06:38.083 --> 00:06:41.323
that y'all continue to
beat the national trend,

00:06:41.323 --> 00:06:42.963
of making sure kids aren't dropping out,

00:06:42.963 --> 00:06:44.786
and getting to college?

00:06:46.053 --> 00:06:46.886
- Yeah. I mean,

00:06:46.886 --> 00:06:48.893
it's a lot of hard work
and I'm just one person

00:06:48.893 --> 00:06:51.193
that works in a giant
orchestra professionals

00:06:51.193 --> 00:06:52.773
that are really thinking about

00:06:52.773 --> 00:06:54.503
how can we make the most of time

00:06:54.503 --> 00:06:57.083
if that time learning has to be virtual.

00:06:57.083 --> 00:06:58.833
Right now we have some students

00:06:58.833 --> 00:06:59.813
that are in-person learning.

00:06:59.813 --> 00:07:02.393
We have some students
that are learning online,

00:07:02.393 --> 00:07:05.573
and we're trying to make the best of it.

00:07:05.573 --> 00:07:07.273
Obviously we're going to have weirdness

00:07:07.273 --> 00:07:08.273
for a very long time,

00:07:08.273 --> 00:07:10.953
but I think the other piece to
this is that school districts

00:07:10.953 --> 00:07:12.603
don't operate in a vacuum.

00:07:12.603 --> 00:07:14.873
We're also subject to a lot of policies

00:07:14.873 --> 00:07:17.793
and this isn't just a Houston ISD thing.

00:07:17.793 --> 00:07:20.373
This is a school districts
across the country thing

00:07:20.373 --> 00:07:22.253
where a lot of districts are wondering,

00:07:22.253 --> 00:07:23.703
well, how will I get funding

00:07:23.703 --> 00:07:26.683
if it makes sense for us
to be virtual for longer?

00:07:26.683 --> 00:07:31.683
Or how do I approach whether
or not I want my schools open

00:07:31.793 --> 00:07:34.863
if our counties are saying
one thing and other folks

00:07:34.863 --> 00:07:36.223
are saying a different thing?

00:07:36.223 --> 00:07:39.053
So it's really a giant,

00:07:39.053 --> 00:07:43.363
big logic tree to figure
out what we're going to do.

00:07:43.363 --> 00:07:44.723
But I would say that us,

00:07:44.723 --> 00:07:46.683
like many districts are really focused on,

00:07:46.683 --> 00:07:47.533
well, how are we going to make sure

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:48.413
that our students are safe?

00:07:48.413 --> 00:07:50.143
How are we going to make
sure that they're receiving

00:07:50.143 --> 00:07:51.613
the best education possible?

00:07:51.613 --> 00:07:53.973
And it's really enthralling
being around colleagues

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:56.143
that are willing to do whatever it takes,

00:07:56.143 --> 00:07:57.873
whatever that looks like to make sure

00:07:57.873 --> 00:07:59.253
that our students are learning,

00:07:59.253 --> 00:08:01.073
they're engaged and they're receiving

00:08:01.073 --> 00:08:03.163
a high quality education.

00:08:03.163 --> 00:08:04.726
And I do want to give
a shout out for things

00:08:04.726 --> 00:08:07.293
that we've been thinking
about for a really long time,

00:08:07.293 --> 00:08:09.303
well, before this pandemic.

00:08:09.303 --> 00:08:12.493
We've really seen the value
in personalized learning

00:08:12.493 --> 00:08:14.353
to supplement what happens in a classroom.

00:08:14.353 --> 00:08:17.783
So that's why we entered in a
partnership with Khan Academy

00:08:17.783 --> 00:08:18.616
a couple of years ago,

00:08:18.616 --> 00:08:21.323
we were the first district
in Texas to sign on

00:08:21.323 --> 00:08:24.223
for such a partnership because
we really saw the beauty

00:08:24.223 --> 00:08:26.313
in what a product like Khan can offer.

00:08:26.313 --> 00:08:27.833
Not that I'm a Khan commercial,

00:08:27.833 --> 00:08:30.733
but like I said, I was a
kid that used Khan Academy,

00:08:30.733 --> 00:08:31.873
so I really believed in it,

00:08:31.873 --> 00:08:33.633
and it's something that
I brought into my work,

00:08:33.633 --> 00:08:37.283
and most of my contemporaries
don't have that background,

00:08:37.283 --> 00:08:39.623
but you know, today what's neat is that

00:08:39.623 --> 00:08:43.493
we give PSATs and SAT exams
for free to our students

00:08:43.493 --> 00:08:44.543
during the school day.

00:08:44.543 --> 00:08:45.973
So we have a ton of data

00:08:45.973 --> 00:08:47.763
as to where kids are at.

00:08:47.763 --> 00:08:49.028
We're able to use that data

00:08:49.028 --> 00:08:51.283
to personalize their learning experience.

00:08:51.283 --> 00:08:53.983
This is something that
we valued for years.

00:08:53.983 --> 00:08:56.403
So, when it came to put
that to the ultimate test,

00:08:56.403 --> 00:08:58.193
we still utilize tools like Khan,

00:08:58.193 --> 00:09:00.173
and what's great about,

00:09:00.173 --> 00:09:03.493
our formal partnership is
being able to look at the data

00:09:03.493 --> 00:09:05.253
to see, okay, well, you know,

00:09:05.253 --> 00:09:07.653
how many minutes are
students using the tool?

00:09:07.653 --> 00:09:11.263
And what we saw and equivocally
is that students were using

00:09:11.263 --> 00:09:12.443
the tool more than they ever had.

00:09:12.443 --> 00:09:14.413
Even we did all these amazing things,

00:09:14.413 --> 00:09:16.533
we had our Khan Athlons and
all those things I mentioned

00:09:16.533 --> 00:09:19.713
for years, but this was
something that they needed,

00:09:19.713 --> 00:09:22.893
and this was something that
we wanted to push on them too,

00:09:22.893 --> 00:09:23.893
because like I said,

00:09:23.893 --> 00:09:25.813
I was worried about how are
we going to get these students

00:09:25.813 --> 00:09:27.583
to college credit that they were going for

00:09:27.583 --> 00:09:29.763
and their advanced placement exams?

00:09:29.763 --> 00:09:33.003
We have a lot of schools
offer associate's degrees

00:09:33.003 --> 00:09:33.923
while in high school,

00:09:33.923 --> 00:09:37.413
how are they going to be able
to finish up their coursework

00:09:37.413 --> 00:09:39.273
to get that degree?

00:09:39.273 --> 00:09:40.993
We have all these students that had all

00:09:40.993 --> 00:09:45.163
these post-secondary ambitions
as a 16 or 17 year old.

00:09:45.163 --> 00:09:48.033
And for all of that to be
kind of put on the whim

00:09:48.033 --> 00:09:51.073
of something none of us have
control over, it's a lot,

00:09:51.073 --> 00:09:52.593
but we're thankful that we have

00:09:52.593 --> 00:09:55.053
incredible college counselors
out there on the field

00:09:55.053 --> 00:09:57.793
that are helping think through
and not to mention advocate.

00:09:57.793 --> 00:10:00.043
I think the advocacy to higher education

00:10:00.043 --> 00:10:01.413
is another piece to this.

00:10:01.413 --> 00:10:04.143
We can talk about the amazing
things going on in classrooms,

00:10:04.143 --> 00:10:06.263
but systemic changes need to happen too,

00:10:06.263 --> 00:10:08.403
and it reminds me a lot about when

00:10:08.403 --> 00:10:09.393
I was leading similar work

00:10:09.393 --> 00:10:12.083
during hurricane Harvey in Houston.

00:10:12.083 --> 00:10:14.693
Another piece of this is
appealing to the universities

00:10:14.693 --> 00:10:17.413
to make sure that they know all the bears

00:10:17.413 --> 00:10:19.963
that especially low income students face

00:10:19.963 --> 00:10:22.403
when they're thinking about
what's next after high school.

00:10:22.403 --> 00:10:23.793
So they really need to meet us halfway

00:10:23.793 --> 00:10:26.013
on a lot of different things,

00:10:26.013 --> 00:10:27.303
and we did that.

00:10:27.303 --> 00:10:29.833
So we did that, we have
the instruction piece,

00:10:29.833 --> 00:10:33.063
and then we had our
digital tools that we use

00:10:33.063 --> 00:10:34.713
when it made sense to use them

00:10:34.713 --> 00:10:36.853
because we had been
investing in it for years.

00:10:36.853 --> 00:10:39.836
So when that way, we were
very pressing our thinking.

00:10:41.063 --> 00:10:42.163
- No, I mean, it makes a lot of sense.

00:10:42.163 --> 00:10:43.803
I mean, just a couple-

00:10:43.803 --> 00:10:46.073
I'm curious about Houston
ISD in particular.

00:10:46.073 --> 00:10:49.033
We've been hearing nationwide
numbers about digital divide,

00:10:49.033 --> 00:10:51.073
about even where the
digital divide is closed

00:10:51.073 --> 00:10:51.973
there's still,

00:10:51.973 --> 00:10:53.783
we've been hearing 5 to 10% of kids

00:10:53.783 --> 00:10:55.743
that were just having trouble reaching.

00:10:55.743 --> 00:10:57.823
What are those numbers
looking like in Houston?

00:10:57.823 --> 00:11:01.913
What percentage of kids
have had trouble accessing,

00:11:01.913 --> 00:11:03.023
digitally?

00:11:03.023 --> 00:11:04.053
How did y'all address it?

00:11:04.053 --> 00:11:06.907
And then even with that support,

00:11:06.907 --> 00:11:09.073
what percentage of the district

00:11:09.073 --> 00:11:11.773
are y'all having trouble engaging with?

00:11:11.773 --> 00:11:14.083
- So I wouldn't have
specific numbers on that,

00:11:14.083 --> 00:11:16.943
and we have incredible
professionals in our IT division

00:11:16.943 --> 00:11:19.233
that really took the lead
on some of the logistics,

00:11:19.233 --> 00:11:22.373
but I will say that it
was a high priority.

00:11:22.373 --> 00:11:23.373
It was a top priority to ensure

00:11:23.373 --> 00:11:27.126
that there wasn't a digital
divide in terms of devices.

00:11:28.143 --> 00:11:31.433
There was a point during this
pandemic where every student

00:11:31.433 --> 00:11:33.693
that requested device got a device,

00:11:33.693 --> 00:11:36.093
and that's something that
we were able to leverage.

00:11:36.093 --> 00:11:38.633
So that way, as instruction
needed to be digital,

00:11:38.633 --> 00:11:39.823
we were able to do that.

00:11:39.823 --> 00:11:41.403
So for that, we're really proud of,

00:11:41.403 --> 00:11:44.983
and then, obviously the
philanthropic community

00:11:44.983 --> 00:11:47.783
and the community in general,

00:11:47.783 --> 00:11:51.973
wanted to do whatever it takes
to really fulfill this need

00:11:51.973 --> 00:11:53.583
for the students in Houston,

00:11:53.583 --> 00:11:56.693
and for those of you who
don't know in Houston ISD

00:11:56.693 --> 00:11:59.843
eight out of 10 students at least qualify

00:11:59.843 --> 00:12:01.863
for free reduced price of lunch,

00:12:01.863 --> 00:12:02.696
and the number of students

00:12:02.696 --> 00:12:06.743
that are going through economic troubles

00:12:06.743 --> 00:12:09.573
certainly increased during this pandemic.

00:12:09.573 --> 00:12:12.863
So, really you have 200
plus thousand students

00:12:12.863 --> 00:12:15.653
that have more needs than
they've ever had before

00:12:15.653 --> 00:12:18.693
when they were ready and
underserved communities.

00:12:18.693 --> 00:12:22.633
So it was a beautiful thing
to see how many organizations,

00:12:22.633 --> 00:12:25.783
public and private in the city
and other folks stepped in

00:12:25.783 --> 00:12:28.783
to make sure that we address
those gaps as much as possible.

00:12:29.803 --> 00:12:31.403
- That's super, super powerful.

00:12:31.403 --> 00:12:32.563
I'm curious, you know, there's,

00:12:32.563 --> 00:12:33.753
and I get this question a lot,

00:12:33.753 --> 00:12:36.153
and I'm fascinated to know
your answers are kind of-

00:12:36.153 --> 00:12:37.846
you're a practitioner.

00:12:40.648 --> 00:12:42.753
There's obviously a lot
of negatives from COVID,

00:12:42.753 --> 00:12:44.183
a lot of them are obvious,

00:12:44.183 --> 00:12:46.404
and there's probably
some ones we'll discover.

00:12:46.404 --> 00:12:48.523
Have there been some silver linings?

00:12:48.523 --> 00:12:51.553
And do you think there's
going to be some long-term

00:12:51.553 --> 00:12:53.563
implications of what we've just
gone through that are going

00:12:53.563 --> 00:12:56.433
to last well beyond 2021, 2022, 2023?

00:12:59.625 --> 00:13:00.733
- You know, I hope so.

00:13:00.733 --> 00:13:02.633
I hope that something's changed.

00:13:02.633 --> 00:13:06.523
I know that all of us want to
go back to January of 2020,

00:13:06.523 --> 00:13:08.743
or December of 2019,

00:13:08.743 --> 00:13:11.653
but I'm someone who really cares deeply

00:13:11.653 --> 00:13:13.143
about urban education,

00:13:13.143 --> 00:13:14.593
and let me tell you that things

00:13:14.593 --> 00:13:16.553
weren't perfect back then either,

00:13:16.553 --> 00:13:19.953
as it relates to education
for students nationwide.

00:13:19.953 --> 00:13:21.203
I think if anything,

00:13:21.203 --> 00:13:24.513
that this crisis really
unveiled a lot of inequity

00:13:24.513 --> 00:13:27.123
and access to things and made
it worse for the students

00:13:27.123 --> 00:13:29.103
that already got the
short end of the stick.

00:13:29.103 --> 00:13:32.073
So I do hope that certain
things can change,

00:13:32.073 --> 00:13:34.293
some of that falls on school districts,

00:13:34.293 --> 00:13:35.983
other that falls on, the state.

00:13:35.983 --> 00:13:38.023
I think that we could re-evaluate the way

00:13:38.023 --> 00:13:41.473
that we provide funds to schools,
especially during crises.

00:13:41.473 --> 00:13:44.993
I think we can think about the role

00:13:44.993 --> 00:13:49.993
of curriculum, the ways that
we can finance technology

00:13:51.053 --> 00:13:52.003
in a sustainable way.

00:13:52.003 --> 00:13:54.033
I hope that there are
a lot of conversations

00:13:54.033 --> 00:13:56.943
that just show that when
things like this happened,

00:13:56.943 --> 00:13:59.513
we really need systemic level change.

00:13:59.513 --> 00:14:00.973
And then there are some practical things

00:14:00.973 --> 00:14:03.283
that I really do hope change too.

00:14:03.283 --> 00:14:05.053
Now some students really struggled

00:14:05.053 --> 00:14:07.373
in the digital learning environment,

00:14:07.373 --> 00:14:09.773
but I do want to point out
something that I think was shared

00:14:09.773 --> 00:14:11.473
on the show by another guest.

00:14:11.473 --> 00:14:13.843
Some students really
thrived in this environment,

00:14:13.843 --> 00:14:17.683
and maybe they didn't anticipate
that, or maybe they did,

00:14:17.683 --> 00:14:21.273
but in some ways, being able
to learn in a virtual way,

00:14:21.273 --> 00:14:23.713
really allowed students to achieve things

00:14:23.713 --> 00:14:25.293
that maybe they wouldn't have otherwise.

00:14:25.293 --> 00:14:29.053
So I think that it's incumbent
upon education officials

00:14:29.053 --> 00:14:31.083
and administrators to think about, well,

00:14:31.083 --> 00:14:33.393
how do we know the type
of learning modalities

00:14:33.393 --> 00:14:35.053
that will help students achieve

00:14:35.053 --> 00:14:37.253
at the highest levels that they can?

00:14:37.253 --> 00:14:40.083
And how can we think about
adjusting our programming

00:14:40.083 --> 00:14:41.753
to making sure that students have access

00:14:41.753 --> 00:14:43.203
to that type of modality?

00:14:43.203 --> 00:14:46.033
You know, I think back to
the one room classroom,

00:14:46.033 --> 00:14:48.256
not that I was ever in
a one room classroom,

00:14:49.343 --> 00:14:50.883
but I think a lot of practices

00:14:50.883 --> 00:14:53.773
that we still use in education nationwide,

00:14:53.773 --> 00:14:55.543
go back to that way of teaching.

00:14:55.543 --> 00:14:57.523
I don't think that classrooms

00:14:57.523 --> 00:15:00.233
across the nation look very
differently than they did

00:15:00.233 --> 00:15:01.453
50 a hundred years ago.

00:15:01.453 --> 00:15:03.773
And isn't this an amazing
time to think about

00:15:03.773 --> 00:15:07.333
what classrooms could
look like moving forward.

00:15:07.333 --> 00:15:09.366
There are all sorts of
little nitpicky things

00:15:09.366 --> 00:15:10.273
that I could pick on,

00:15:10.273 --> 00:15:11.583
but I'll give one concrete example

00:15:11.583 --> 00:15:12.893
that I'm passionate about.

00:15:12.893 --> 00:15:16.433
So just as an individual I'm
very passionate about access

00:15:16.433 --> 00:15:18.430
to community college,

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.073
and one thing that always boggled my mind

00:15:21.073 --> 00:15:24.583
was that community colleges
often put requirements

00:15:24.583 --> 00:15:27.163
for students to take
courses at the college.

00:15:27.163 --> 00:15:29.923
They need to score a
certain number on the test,

00:15:29.923 --> 00:15:32.103
like an SAT or whatever that is,

00:15:32.103 --> 00:15:35.013
otherwise they'll need to
go into remedial coursework.

00:15:35.013 --> 00:15:37.653
And the problem with that is when you look

00:15:37.653 --> 00:15:41.023
at open enrollment institutions
of higher education,

00:15:41.023 --> 00:15:43.423
the graduation rates are abysmal.

00:15:43.423 --> 00:15:45.603
Sometimes they're in the single digits,

00:15:45.603 --> 00:15:48.223
so whatever the system is,
it's not working in some ways,

00:15:48.223 --> 00:15:51.083
I think policies like
that can gatekeep students

00:15:51.083 --> 00:15:53.443
from pursuing a degree
that may have nothing

00:15:53.443 --> 00:15:55.143
to do with math or reading.

00:15:55.143 --> 00:15:57.823
So isn't this an opportunity to say, "Hey,

00:15:57.823 --> 00:16:00.223
how about we change
that and figure out ways

00:16:00.223 --> 00:16:03.683
to get students taking courses
that excite them right now,

00:16:03.683 --> 00:16:05.003
without that course right now."

00:16:05.003 --> 00:16:07.103
And that's what happened during COVID,

00:16:07.103 --> 00:16:09.313
a lot of community
colleges across the nation

00:16:09.313 --> 00:16:10.723
weighed those requirements simply

00:16:10.723 --> 00:16:12.973
because students couldn't
access those exams.

00:16:13.883 --> 00:16:15.850
But I would be curious to know
what that data looks like.

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:17.703
And you may see more students thriving

00:16:17.703 --> 00:16:19.793
in their degree programs than ever before.

00:16:19.793 --> 00:16:23.153
And that's because we were
forced to do something

00:16:23.153 --> 00:16:25.783
that we had been doing
just to do something,

00:16:25.783 --> 00:16:28.063
and we're doing it
completely different now.

00:16:28.063 --> 00:16:30.343
Things like that, I think
are all over the place,

00:16:30.343 --> 00:16:31.633
the opportunities, right?

00:16:31.633 --> 00:16:34.703
Not just in education, but in
industries across the field,

00:16:34.703 --> 00:16:37.053
I think we're reevaluating
what remote learning,

00:16:37.053 --> 00:16:39.573
and what remote working can look like.

00:16:39.573 --> 00:16:42.933
Imagine if school
districts and organizations

00:16:42.933 --> 00:16:45.323
really stopped thinking
about who's the best person

00:16:45.323 --> 00:16:48.283
for the job close to our office.

00:16:48.283 --> 00:16:50.673
How about who's the best person for a job

00:16:50.673 --> 00:16:52.983
that lives in the entire United States,

00:16:52.983 --> 00:16:56.011
especially for small districts
and small organizations.

00:16:56.011 --> 00:16:57.593
I think that could be game changing.

00:16:57.593 --> 00:16:59.133
So those are just a couple of examples.

00:16:59.133 --> 00:17:01.553
I really hope something
can changed for the better

00:17:01.553 --> 00:17:04.453
when it comes to our students
and underserved communities.

00:17:05.663 --> 00:17:06.853
- The last point you made is actually-

00:17:06.853 --> 00:17:08.743
I mean, all of those were
really interesting points.

00:17:08.743 --> 00:17:09.923
That last point you made was fascinating

00:17:09.923 --> 00:17:11.223
because I actually never
thought about that.

00:17:11.223 --> 00:17:13.003
Obviously, a lot of-

00:17:13.003 --> 00:17:15.723
we as Khan Academy is actually
a distributed organization.

00:17:15.723 --> 00:17:18.863
We have a headquarters and
we're kind of thinking about

00:17:18.863 --> 00:17:21.333
to what degree how that
will be afterwards,

00:17:21.333 --> 00:17:23.603
but I hadn't fully
thought about that notion

00:17:23.603 --> 00:17:24.793
that even school districts,

00:17:24.793 --> 00:17:26.653
which you've used, a very local thing,

00:17:26.653 --> 00:17:30.513
even those might be able to
tap talent at a national level

00:17:30.513 --> 00:17:33.053
of which could be quite powerful.

00:17:33.053 --> 00:17:34.093
And you know, some of
what you talked about,

00:17:34.093 --> 00:17:35.433
community college requirements,

00:17:35.433 --> 00:17:36.283
I agree with what you're saying.

00:17:36.283 --> 00:17:37.653
It will be interesting to see the data

00:17:37.653 --> 00:17:39.103
when they waive those requirements.

00:17:39.103 --> 00:17:41.393
And also one of my pet projects

00:17:41.393 --> 00:17:43.863
is trying to figure out
how we can get kids,

00:17:43.863 --> 00:17:46.703
the credit for things like not
even just the remedial work,

00:17:46.703 --> 00:17:47.893
but the college algebra

00:17:47.893 --> 00:17:51.843
or that first English
language or writing course

00:17:51.843 --> 00:17:53.093
before they graduate high school.

00:17:53.093 --> 00:17:55.803
But that we could have a
whole conversation on that.

00:17:55.803 --> 00:17:58.343
We're getting questions
incoming on social media

00:17:58.343 --> 00:18:00.453
and anyone watching, feel
free to put your questions

00:18:00.453 --> 00:18:01.843
in for Jharett. I will try to get

00:18:01.843 --> 00:18:03.163
to as many of them as possible,

00:18:03.163 --> 00:18:06.043
but we're getting a lot of
questions about people curious

00:18:06.043 --> 00:18:07.873
about your career pathway.

00:18:07.873 --> 00:18:08.893
I guess I alluded to,

00:18:08.893 --> 00:18:10.993
it seems like you're on a rocket ship

00:18:10.993 --> 00:18:13.793
in terms how quickly you're progressing.

00:18:13.793 --> 00:18:17.593
The first question is from
Susanna Garcia Dominguez

00:18:17.593 --> 00:18:21.370
from YouTube, who is a
regular on this show.

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:22.683
"Hello, Dr. Bryantt,

00:18:22.683 --> 00:18:25.183
how did you know that you
wanted to go into administration

00:18:25.183 --> 00:18:26.853
instead of being a classroom teacher?"

00:18:26.853 --> 00:18:28.403
And I know you started
as a classroom teacher,

00:18:28.403 --> 00:18:30.443
but tell us how that pathway works.

00:18:30.443 --> 00:18:32.283
I think most people know
how to become a teacher.

00:18:32.283 --> 00:18:33.593
Most people don't know how to become

00:18:33.593 --> 00:18:35.016
an assistant superintendent.

00:18:36.093 --> 00:18:39.443
- Yeah. I think that's a
really a great question

00:18:39.443 --> 00:18:41.133
because here's the thing,

00:18:41.133 --> 00:18:44.463
I was one of many people
that went to college thinking

00:18:44.463 --> 00:18:48.213
that my only path to success
would be to be pre-med

00:18:48.213 --> 00:18:51.270
or to think of these
very conventional things.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:52.823
And I was going to do that.

00:18:52.823 --> 00:18:53.973
I went to a place-

00:18:53.973 --> 00:18:57.523
- My mother still thinks
my only path to success

00:18:57.523 --> 00:19:00.143
is pre-med, but anyway,
sorry, I interrupted.

00:19:00.143 --> 00:19:00.976
- No, it's fine.

00:19:00.976 --> 00:19:04.403
But the thing is that I didn't
know that my job existed.

00:19:04.403 --> 00:19:05.903
I didn't know that teaching existed.

00:19:05.903 --> 00:19:08.043
I took a course in college
that changed my life,

00:19:08.043 --> 00:19:10.743
it was called Solving the
American Education Crisis,

00:19:10.743 --> 00:19:12.753
and it was about all these inequities

00:19:12.753 --> 00:19:14.273
that happen in urban school systems

00:19:14.273 --> 00:19:15.373
that I hadn't thought about.

00:19:15.373 --> 00:19:17.813
That was my experience, I
thought schools were fine.

00:19:17.813 --> 00:19:18.673
They're not fine.

00:19:18.673 --> 00:19:21.953
And I felt really compelled
to go into a classroom

00:19:21.953 --> 00:19:24.485
and to experience that firsthand.

00:19:24.485 --> 00:19:27.563
And then I think the thing
that changed the game for me

00:19:27.563 --> 00:19:29.773
was that I wasn't so
much thinking about being

00:19:29.773 --> 00:19:32.723
in the classroom versus
not being in the classroom.

00:19:32.723 --> 00:19:35.253
I was thinking about what gives me energy?

00:19:35.253 --> 00:19:37.113
And I would say the turning point for me,

00:19:37.113 --> 00:19:39.823
because I did have a lot of steps between

00:19:39.823 --> 00:19:42.683
what I do right now, and when
I was a classroom teacher,

00:19:42.683 --> 00:19:45.963
but the lesson I learned
remains exactly the same.

00:19:45.963 --> 00:19:48.463
So I was transitioning
out of the classroom

00:19:48.463 --> 00:19:50.603
and I thought that I'd go off to be

00:19:50.603 --> 00:19:52.193
what they call management consultant,

00:19:52.193 --> 00:19:55.943
basically put together
spreadsheets and PowerPoint decks

00:19:55.943 --> 00:19:56.776
for a living,

00:19:56.776 --> 00:19:59.283
but really expensive and really
important to some people.

00:19:59.283 --> 00:20:02.243
But anyway, I met somebody
that changed my life

00:20:02.243 --> 00:20:05.123
because he was founding an organization

00:20:05.123 --> 00:20:08.873
that really targeted those
high achieving students

00:20:08.873 --> 00:20:11.863
in underserved schools to help get them

00:20:11.863 --> 00:20:16.033
to highly selective universities
like Stanford or Harvard

00:20:16.033 --> 00:20:19.543
or wherever that is because
those schools full rides

00:20:19.543 --> 00:20:22.733
to students who are low income.

00:20:22.733 --> 00:20:25.963
So anyway, I chose to drop
everything that I was doing

00:20:25.963 --> 00:20:27.823
to make that program work.

00:20:27.823 --> 00:20:30.463
And I think what I learned from
that experience was you need

00:20:30.463 --> 00:20:32.603
to figure out where you
get that energy from.

00:20:32.603 --> 00:20:34.533
I never felt that energy again,

00:20:34.533 --> 00:20:37.593
or at least as strongly as
when I spoke to the founder

00:20:37.593 --> 00:20:40.193
of that organization about
that special program,

00:20:40.193 --> 00:20:42.853
but that energy, that feeling of desire,

00:20:42.853 --> 00:20:46.203
that feeling of wanting to
make a difference in this way,

00:20:46.203 --> 00:20:47.573
I just had to pursue it.

00:20:47.573 --> 00:20:50.363
And it ended up looking
different over the years,

00:20:50.363 --> 00:20:51.893
I realized that it wasn't so much

00:20:51.893 --> 00:20:54.123
that particular program
I was passionate about,

00:20:54.123 --> 00:20:57.343
it was really speaking up
for the underdogs in society

00:20:57.343 --> 00:20:58.863
and making sure they get their fair shake

00:20:58.863 --> 00:21:00.603
and they get resources.

00:21:00.603 --> 00:21:02.343
So then it looked like putting counselors

00:21:02.343 --> 00:21:03.313
in all the high schools.

00:21:03.313 --> 00:21:05.593
That looked like thinking
about how do we get

00:21:05.593 --> 00:21:08.223
as many low-income students
as possible to pass

00:21:08.223 --> 00:21:09.803
an advanced placement exam.

00:21:09.803 --> 00:21:12.283
Then it looked like, you know, XYZ.

00:21:12.283 --> 00:21:15.233
But the common denominator
is I stopped listening

00:21:15.233 --> 00:21:18.203
to incredible people that I really admire

00:21:18.203 --> 00:21:19.313
because there are still
people that tell me

00:21:19.313 --> 00:21:22.623
I should be doing X and Y,
but I always follow my heart.

00:21:22.623 --> 00:21:24.913
And I know that sounds trite,

00:21:24.913 --> 00:21:29.003
but I was very lucky that
something spoke to my heart,

00:21:29.003 --> 00:21:31.613
and for the first time I
stopped following my gut

00:21:31.613 --> 00:21:34.313
and I just did it and it
could have totally failed,

00:21:34.313 --> 00:21:35.233
(chuckles)

00:21:35.233 --> 00:21:36.103
but it didn't.

00:21:36.103 --> 00:21:39.186
I truly think that that's what
made the difference for me.

00:21:40.403 --> 00:21:42.123
- And on the other side of that coin,

00:21:42.123 --> 00:21:43.663
what are the things that
you think you're doing?

00:21:43.663 --> 00:21:45.723
And I know this is a
hard question to answer,

00:21:45.723 --> 00:21:47.843
but what do you think of
the things that you're doing

00:21:47.843 --> 00:21:49.483
that have given you that success,

00:21:49.483 --> 00:21:51.773
that have allowed you to be an effective,

00:21:51.773 --> 00:21:54.193
not just educator, but
administrator as well,

00:21:54.193 --> 00:21:55.843
and for people to recognize that?

00:21:56.742 --> 00:21:58.743
- I think the number one thing is you have

00:21:58.743 --> 00:22:00.323
to be around people that are willing

00:22:00.323 --> 00:22:02.323
to take a chance on you.

00:22:02.323 --> 00:22:05.433
Even if I were to consider
different roles in education

00:22:05.433 --> 00:22:06.783
or whatever it is elsewhere,

00:22:06.783 --> 00:22:08.753
some people aren't willing
to take a chance on somebody

00:22:08.753 --> 00:22:10.933
that thinks about things unconventionally.

00:22:10.933 --> 00:22:12.713
It goes back to what I mentioned before,

00:22:12.713 --> 00:22:15.403
I'm probably one of the only
contemporaries in my building

00:22:15.403 --> 00:22:18.023
that use Khan Academy as
a high school student,

00:22:18.023 --> 00:22:20.483
obviously for obvious reasons.

00:22:20.483 --> 00:22:24.493
But I've been very fortunate
that I've had mentors,

00:22:24.493 --> 00:22:28.223
I've had an amazing boss
who really been willing

00:22:28.223 --> 00:22:30.853
to take a chance on somebody
who maybe didn't have

00:22:30.853 --> 00:22:33.543
the most conventional
background for something,

00:22:33.543 --> 00:22:35.073
but they saw something, they saw a spark,

00:22:35.073 --> 00:22:38.283
they saw potential and they're
willing to take a risk.

00:22:38.283 --> 00:22:41.673
So that something that I think
made the difference for me

00:22:41.673 --> 00:22:43.973
was that I did have
somebody who signed off

00:22:43.973 --> 00:22:45.316
on me taking on this job.

00:22:45.316 --> 00:22:47.040
I think I was 25 at the time.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.990
And some people would say that's crazy.

00:22:49.873 --> 00:22:51.943
I recently was-

00:22:51.943 --> 00:22:53.883
I made the Forbes under 30 list

00:22:53.883 --> 00:22:55.663
and I finally got some Twitter haters.

00:22:55.663 --> 00:22:58.373
Like I felt like a mini
celebrity for like a minute.

00:22:58.373 --> 00:23:00.423
And I saw some of the things
that were put out there

00:23:00.423 --> 00:23:01.416
and it's like, wow.

00:23:02.473 --> 00:23:05.793
But I had mentors that really saw beyond

00:23:05.793 --> 00:23:08.533
what people will say on
Twitter and that sort of thing,

00:23:08.533 --> 00:23:10.753
and they just really believe
in investing in somebody,

00:23:10.753 --> 00:23:11.803
and I wasn't perfect,

00:23:11.803 --> 00:23:14.833
and I had people that were
willing to do what it takes

00:23:14.833 --> 00:23:18.103
to make me see things that
I didn't see in myself.

00:23:18.103 --> 00:23:19.563
So, I value that,

00:23:19.563 --> 00:23:21.963
I really aspire to be
that for somebody one day.

00:23:22.953 --> 00:23:24.253
- Now, that's incredibly powerful,

00:23:24.253 --> 00:23:26.173
and I think it's going to be
inspiring to a lot of people

00:23:26.173 --> 00:23:28.543
who are watching, people of all ages.

00:23:28.543 --> 00:23:30.863
And the time we have left and
maybe this kind of combines

00:23:30.863 --> 00:23:33.693
both parts of our
conversation, your journey,

00:23:33.693 --> 00:23:36.143
and also the journey
of education generally.

00:23:36.143 --> 00:23:38.993
What are the things that
you're most worried about

00:23:38.993 --> 00:23:41.463
right now, either in your own life

00:23:41.463 --> 00:23:44.343
or in education or in your job?

00:23:44.343 --> 00:23:46.483
And what are the things that
you're most hopeful about?

00:23:46.483 --> 00:23:48.843
And any other advice you
have for those watching,

00:23:48.843 --> 00:23:51.173
especially the young people out there.

00:23:51.173 --> 00:23:52.093
- You know, I'll keep it real.

00:23:52.093 --> 00:23:54.543
I feel I'm a little worried
that people are going

00:23:54.543 --> 00:23:58.163
to really romanticize what
it was before this pandemic.

00:23:58.163 --> 00:23:59.833
I think that we're all
really wanting things

00:23:59.833 --> 00:24:01.583
to go back to normal,

00:24:01.583 --> 00:24:03.803
and I just hope that we don't
over-correct and go back

00:24:03.803 --> 00:24:07.383
to the things that
really started inequity.

00:24:07.383 --> 00:24:10.123
Remember, inequity didn't
start with the pandemic,

00:24:10.123 --> 00:24:11.053
it was already there,

00:24:11.053 --> 00:24:13.173
and that's probably a theme that comes up

00:24:13.173 --> 00:24:15.073
in a lot of different fields.

00:24:15.073 --> 00:24:18.783
It really accelerated things
that we're already there.

00:24:18.783 --> 00:24:23.783
So the flip side, the
positive thing about crises,

00:24:23.813 --> 00:24:26.543
whatever that may look
like, is it's an opportunity

00:24:26.543 --> 00:24:28.463
to put a shock in the system,

00:24:28.463 --> 00:24:31.703
to reflect and to do things better.

00:24:31.703 --> 00:24:34.073
So while I'm very concerned
that people are going

00:24:34.073 --> 00:24:36.683
to want to go back to the
way that we did things,

00:24:36.683 --> 00:24:37.573
including the things

00:24:37.573 --> 00:24:41.923
that really didn't help
propel our underserved,

00:24:41.923 --> 00:24:43.883
I am really excited about this idea

00:24:43.883 --> 00:24:45.543
that we'll look at technology differently,

00:24:45.543 --> 00:24:47.933
that we'll think about
education differently,

00:24:47.933 --> 00:24:50.463
that we'll learn how to
operate school systems,

00:24:50.463 --> 00:24:53.043
school funding, school
finance at state level,

00:24:53.043 --> 00:24:54.743
even federal policy.

00:24:54.743 --> 00:24:56.576
There are so many things
that I want to advocate for

00:24:56.576 --> 00:24:58.363
that I don't think people
would have listened to

00:24:58.363 --> 00:25:02.293
for two seconds had this pandemic
not put it on their radar

00:25:02.293 --> 00:25:03.126
as an issue.

00:25:03.126 --> 00:25:04.023
And I know we're running out of time,

00:25:04.023 --> 00:25:05.443
but maybe next time I can talk about

00:25:05.443 --> 00:25:06.893
those particular things.

00:25:06.893 --> 00:25:08.516
So stay tuned, my friends.

00:25:09.733 --> 00:25:10.603
- We look forward to that.

00:25:10.603 --> 00:25:12.833
Well, Jharett, thanks for joining,

00:25:12.833 --> 00:25:15.373
and I know there's a lot of work.

00:25:15.373 --> 00:25:17.443
You have your work cut out at Houston ISD,

00:25:17.443 --> 00:25:20.783
but it sounds like y'all
are doing better than most.

00:25:20.783 --> 00:25:23.383
And I think we're going
to see a lot more from you

00:25:23.383 --> 00:25:26.263
in the education world
doing really great things.

00:25:26.263 --> 00:25:29.158
- Thank you Sal, it was
a pleasure to be on.

00:25:29.158 --> 00:25:30.683
- Thanks for joining.

00:25:30.683 --> 00:25:32.693
Well, everyone, hopefully you
enjoyed that conversation.

00:25:32.693 --> 00:25:34.023
I always think we're
like 10 minutes into it

00:25:34.023 --> 00:25:34.973
and I look at the clock and I'm like,

00:25:34.973 --> 00:25:36.893
Oh wow, we're almost
out of time, (chuckles)

00:25:36.893 --> 00:25:38.433
time flies when we're having fun.

00:25:38.433 --> 00:25:39.473
But hopefully you enjoyed

00:25:39.473 --> 00:25:41.793
that conversation with Dr. Bryantt,

00:25:41.793 --> 00:25:44.033
and I am always blown away

00:25:44.033 --> 00:25:46.773
that he actually used Khan
Academy in high school,

00:25:46.773 --> 00:25:47.673
in college.

00:25:47.673 --> 00:25:49.983
So, one good we're having impact,

00:25:49.983 --> 00:25:54.259
but we're getting older than
some of us would like to admit.

00:25:54.259 --> 00:25:55.313
(chuckles) But we'll see you all,

00:25:55.313 --> 00:25:57.903
have everyone have a good
rest of your week and weekend,

00:25:57.903 --> 00:25:59.283
and we'll see you next Tuesday.

00:25:59.283 --> 00:26:01.413
We're going to have a
real good conversation.

00:26:01.413 --> 00:26:04.303
Continuing the conversation about families

00:26:04.303 --> 00:26:07.513
and their ability to
engage during the pandemic.

00:26:07.513 --> 00:26:08.713
But I will see you then.

